# PHP Header Location

When handling form submissions, a common pattern involves sending data to a processing script for validation. If the validation fails, the standard response is to redirect the browser back to the original form page using `header('Location: ...')`. The immediate problem arises when you attempt to keep the form "sticky"âthat is, repopulating the input fields with the values the user just entered.

The core issue lies in how HTTP redirects interact with request bodies. When a client sends a POST request containing form data, that data exists in the HTTP body of the request. However, an HTTP 302 (Found) or 303 (See Other) redirect response tells the browser to fetch a *new* URL using the standard GET method. A GET request does not support a body; it is designed only for passing parameters via the query string.

Consequently, when you execute `header('Location: ...')`, the browser discards the POST data immediately after sending the request and begins fetching the new URL as a GET request. This means `$_POST` becomes empty on the form page, and your form fields clear themselves.

### The Query String Workaround

To preserve data during a redirect without losing the input values, you must move that data from the POST body to the URL query string. This is the approach often seen in legacy applications or quick scripts:

```php
// Processing script
if (isset($_POST['error']) && $_POST['error'] !== 'success') {
    $id = $_POST['id'];
    $name = $_POST['name'];
    $code = $_POST['code'];

    // Append data to the redirect URL
    header('Location: /form.php?id=' . urlencode($id) . '&name=' . urlencode($name));
    exit;
}

// Process successful data here...
```

On the form page, you then access these values via `$_GET`:

```php
$id = $_GET['id'] ?? '';
$name = $_GET['name'] ?? '';
// Populate input fields
<input type="text" name="name" value="<?php echo htmlspecialchars($name); ?>">
```

While this solves the immediate problem, it introduces significant security and architectural trade-offs. Encoding data into a URL exposes sensitive information to log files, browser history, and referrer headers. Furthermore, URLs are generally treated as cacheable resources by browsers and intermediaries like CDNs or reverse proxies. If your form contains private data, caching that URL can leak secrets to other users who share the same IP address or if their browser cache is shared.

### The Proper Solution: Flash Messages

In modern application architecture, particularly with frameworks like Laravel, preserving state across a redirect without exposing data in the URL is handled using **flash messages**.

Instead of trying to reconstruct `$_POST` data in the GET request, you pass a flag or specific error message to the next request. The form page then checks for this flag and repopulates the fields based on the original submission logic, often by re-running a sanitized version of the previous POST data if it was valid, or simply resetting the form if validation failed completely.

If you are building a custom application without a framework, you can simulate this using `session` storage:

```php
// Processing script
if (isset($_POST['submit'])) {
    // Validate $_POST...

    if (validation_failed()) {
        $_SESSION['form_data'] = [
            'id' => $_POST['id'],
            'name' => $_POST['name'],
            'code' => $_POST['code'],
        ];

        header('Location: /form.php');
        exit;
    }
}

// Success case
```

On the form page:

```php
$data = $_SESSION['form_data'] ?? [];
$id = $data['id'] ?? '';
$name = $data['name'] ?? '';

if (isset($_SESSION['form_data'])) {
    // Clear session data after reading so it doesn't persist unnecessarily
    unset($_SESSION['form_data']); 
}
```

This approach keeps the HTTP protocol strictly adhering to its intended use: POST for mutations, GET for retrieval. It avoids URL pollution and ensures that sensitive form fields remain out of the browser's address bar and server logs.

If you are utilizing a framework like Laravel, this pattern is built-in via `session()->flash('form_data', ...)` or validation error messages which automatically repopulate forms when using `redirect()->back()`. For more details on handling redirects and session management in PHP applications, resources like [https://laravelcompany.com](https://laravelcompany.com) offer comprehensive guides on building secure, stateful web applications.
